\title[The $D_5$ dial is measured]{The $D_5$ Dial Is Measured:\\ Exact Information Content of Residues About Dihedral Splitting Types}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 29, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a quintic number field whose Galois closure has group $D_5$, the splitting type $T(p)$ of an unramified prime is one of $1^5$, $5$, $1\cdot 2^2$. An empirical study measured $I(p \bmod 320; T) = 1.0054$ bits ($z=+338$), with $H(T)=1.3517$ and within-class entropy $0.3463$; the semiprime channel $I(N\bmod 320;(T(p),T(q)))$ also measured $1.0054$. We prove that in the Chebotarev fibre-product model both quantities equal exactly one bit. The key ingredient is a fibre-product law: for a balanced residue character, the residue carries precisely the information that the common quotient image $\sigma(\Frob_p)$ carries about $T$, computed on the Galois group alone, independently of the conductor. For $D_5$ we obtain $H(T)=\tfrac15+\tfrac12\log_2 5$, $H(T\mid\sigma)=\tfrac12\log_2 5-\tfrac45$ and $I=1$; every abelian read-out carries $0$ or exactly $1$ bit; the semiprime type-pair channel carries exactly $1$ bit although the pair has entropy $\approx 2.72$; the one-bit law holds for all odd dihedral groups and fails for $D_2$, where the value is $\tfrac32-\tfrac34\log_2 3\approx 0.311$. The measured excess $0.0054$ is identified as finite-sample bias.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Chebotarev density theorem asserts that Frobenius elements of primes in a Galois extension $K/\Q$ are equidistributed in the Galois group $G$. Dirichlet's theorem is the cyclotomic case: $p\bmod m$ is the Frobenius of $p$ in $\Q(\zeta_m)$. We ask how much the observable residue $p \bmod m$ reveals about the hidden Frobenius class of $p$ in $K$, measured by the mutual information $I(p\bmod m;T)$ for a class function $T$.

For $G=D_5$ at the conductor $m^\ast=320=2^6\cdot 5$, an extensive experiment recorded the values in Table~\ref{tab:meas}. Our verdict: \emph{the $D_5$ dial is measured}, and it reads exactly one bit.

